    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d386">

                <pb facs="0383" n="267" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg267"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 837.00/878.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the
                            Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 323.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-07-02">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 2, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: While there have been no radical
                    developments since Estenoz was killed, I think I may safely say that the
                    situation has improved to a remarked degree, and that a very encouraging change
                    is manifest in public confidence. It is, of course, impossible to predict with
                    certainty what may happen so long as Ivonet and other leaders contrive to elude
                    capture, but it is now generally believed that the Government has the upper hand
                    and will, with a proper display of energy, be able completely to crush the
                    revolt.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">A. M. Beaupré</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
